&lt;p&gt;Check for unfamiliar sign-ins, unexpected changes, and security alerts. Investigate activity you do not recognize.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review Third-Party Access&lt;br&gt;
Periodically check which applications have permission to access your Google Account. Remove permissions for applications you no longer use or trust.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Be Careful With Suspicious Messages&lt;br&gt;
Avoid opening unexpected attachments or following links that request passwords or verification codes. Visit official Google services directly when checking your account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Protect Devices&lt;br&gt;
Keep your browser and operating system updated. Use a screen lock on personal devices and sign out when using a shared computer.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;For more information, visit Google Account Security.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gmail Privacy and Digital Identity&lt;br&gt;
An email address can become an important part of a person's digital identity. It may be used for communication, account notifications, document sharing, and recovery for other services.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Protecting that identity requires attention to the information associated with the account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Useful privacy practices include:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Avoid sharing passwords and verification codes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review personal information associated with the account.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Check forwarding rules and filters for unexpected changes.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review devices and applications with account access.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Avoid storing sensitive information unnecessarily in email.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use appropriate sharing permissions for documents.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Be cautious when opening files from unfamiliar senders.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When an email account has been used by another person, privacy risks can affect both the previous owner and anyone whose information appears in the account. Maintaining an account you legitimately control is a safer approach.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Organizing Gmail for Everyday Productivity&lt;br&gt;
A well-organized inbox can make an email account more useful regardless of when it was created.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use Labels&lt;br&gt;
Gmail labels can help categorize messages related to work, personal communication, receipts, subscriptions, and projects.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Create Filters&lt;br&gt;
Filters can automatically organize incoming messages based on selected criteria. Review them periodically to ensure that important email is not being misplaced.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Archive Unneeded Messages&lt;br&gt;
Archiving can reduce inbox clutter while preserving messages that may be useful later.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review Subscriptions&lt;br&gt;
Unsubscribe from unwanted mailing lists when appropriate. Report suspicious messages rather than responding to them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Maintain Contact Information&lt;br&gt;
Keep contact details organized and respect other people's privacy when storing or sharing their information.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Separate Different Activities&lt;br&gt;
Where appropriate, use separate email addresses for personal correspondence, professional communication, and newsletters. This can make organization and privacy management easier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Gmail for Professional and Business Communication&lt;br&gt;
Gmail can support many legitimate communication needs, from individual correspondence to professional workflows.&lt;/p&gt;

